WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.020
Some pages should not be embedded inside frames, iframes, objects, or similar containers.

2
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:12.280
Framing controls help the application tell the browser whether a page may be embedded by another page.

3
00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:24.700
This matters for account pages, administrative pages, transaction pages, approval workflows, and other sensitive interactions where the user experience should stay under the application's control.

4
00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:30.100
X-Frame-Options is an older but still commonly encountered header for frame restrictions.

5
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:35.560
It can express a small set of choices, such as denying framing or allowing same-origin framing.

6
00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:44.300
Many existing applications and platforms still include it as part of a baseline, but it is limited compared with modern CSP-based controls.

7
00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:49.940
CSP frame-ancestors provides a more flexible framing control for browsers that support it.

8
00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:53.640
It can define which parent origins may embed the page.

9
00:00:53.740 --> 00:01:02.620
That is useful when a page must be embedded by a specific trusted parent, such as a known portal or internal shell, but should not be embeddable elsewhere.

10
00:01:02.900 --> 00:01:05.220
Embedding decisions should be intentional.

11
00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.640
Some applications need no embedding.

12
00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:11.100
Some need same-origin embedding for internal layouts.

13
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:13.600
Some need a specific parent origin.

14
00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:17.700
Those choices should be based on business requirements and reviewed by page type.

15
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:25.000
Admin pages, account pages, transaction pages, and high-impact workflow pages usually deserve extra care.

16
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:29.920
Framing controls do not replace authorization or CSRF protection.

17
00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:32.340
They help with browser embedding behavior.

18
00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:42.360
The application still needs to authenticate users, authorize actions, validate state-changing requests, protect sensitive data, and log important events.

19
00:01:42.460 --> 00:01:46.620
Framing headers are one layer in a broader browser and application security model.
